{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T04:50:58.010Z","ordinal":37881,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a10e2a-6616-7a42-8b17-50e4af22487f","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-3a5e656d-5160-4740-898d-4bfe33b16630","process_id":"55695","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rg --files node_modules/.bun | rg 'workers-types.*/index.d.ts$|Bridge.ts$' | head -12; sed -n '1,110p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Bridge.ts; rg -n 'Scope|Request|remoteAddress' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Bridge.ts | head -25"],"cwd":"file:///Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"list_files","cmd":"rg --files node_modules/.bun","path":".bun"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg 'workers-types.*/index.d.ts$|Bridge.ts$'","query":"workers-types.*/index.d.ts$|Bridge.ts$","path":null},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,110p' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Bridge.ts","name":"Bridge.ts","path":"node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Bridge.ts"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'Scope|Request|remoteAddress' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Bridge.ts","query":"Scope|Request|remoteAddress","path":"Bridge.ts"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"/**\n * Runtime bridge factories and RPC helpers. This entry must not depend on\n * resource providers or local development tooling, even through dynamic imports.\n */\nexport * from \"./Fetcher.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/InferEnv.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/Rpc.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/RpcAsync.ts\";\n\n// ── runtime bridge factories ──\nexport { makeDurableObjectBridge } from \"./Workers/DurableObjectBridge.ts\";\nexport { makeWorkerBridge } from \"./Workers/WorkerBridge.ts\";\nexport { withSqlMigrations } from \"./Workers/SqlMigrationsRuntime.ts\";\nexport { makeWorkflowBridge } from \"./Workflows/WorkflowBridge.ts\";\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"/**\n * Runtime bridge factories and RPC helpers. This entry must not depend on\n * resource providers or local development tooling, even through dynamic imports.\n */\nexport * from \"./Fetcher.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/InferEnv.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/Rpc.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/RpcAsync.ts\";\n\n// ── runtime bridge factories ──\nexport { makeDurableObjectBridge } from \"./Workers/DurableObjectBridge.ts\";\nexport { makeWorkerBridge } from \"./Workers/WorkerBridge.ts\";\nexport { withSqlMigrations } from \"./Workers/SqlMigrationsRuntime.ts\";\nexport { makeWorkflowBridge } from \"./Workflows/WorkflowBridge.ts\";\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":7709},"formatted_output":"/**\n * Runtime bridge factories and RPC helpers. This entry must not depend on\n * resource providers or local development tooling, even through dynamic imports.\n */\nexport * from \"./Fetcher.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/InferEnv.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/Rpc.ts\";\nexport * from \"./Workers/RpcAsync.ts\";\n\n// ── runtime bridge factories ──\nexport { makeDurableObjectBridge } from \"./Workers/DurableObjectBridge.ts\";\nexport { makeWorkerBridge } from \"./Workers/WorkerBridge.ts\";\nexport { withSqlMigrations } from \"./Workers/SqlMigrationsRuntime.ts\";\nexport { makeWorkflowBridge } from \"./Workflows/WorkflowBridge.ts\";\n"},"started_at_ms":1791262258010,"completed_at_ms":1791262258010}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T04:50:58.061Z","ordinal":37882,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a10f8c-f38d-7272-8950-8ca51879cdc6","call_id":"call_KR8qgtqwRXuuTLUXx2PyzRGL","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.8 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Cloudflare HTTP headers · Cloudflare Fundamentals docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/reference/http-headers/)\nciteturn23view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view0\",\"pattern\":\"CF-Worker\"}); Total lines: 417\nL232: ### CF-Connecting-IPv6\nL233: \nL234: Cloudflare provides cite140†free IPv6 support to all domains without requiring additional configuration or hardware. To support migrating to IPv6, Cloudflare's cite141†Pseudo IPv4 provides an IPv6 to IPv4 translation service for all Cloudflare domains.\nL235: \nL236: If Pseudo IPv4 is set to `Overwrite Headers` - Cloudflare overwrites the existing `Cf-Connecting-IP` and `X-Forwarded-For` headers with a pseudo IPv4 address while preserving the real IPv6 address in `CF-Connecting-IPv6` header.\nL237: ### CF-EW-Via\nL238: \nL239: This header is used for loop detection, similar to the `CDN-Loop` cite142†header ↗︎†blog.cloudflare.com .\nL240: \nL241: ### CF-Pseudo-IPv4\nL242: \nL243: If cite141†Pseudo IPv4 is set to `Add Header` - Cloudflare automatically adds the `CF-Pseudo-IPv4` header with a Class E IPv4 address hashed from the original IPv6 address.\nL244: ### True-Client-IP (Enterprise plan only)\nL245: \nL246: `True-Client-IP` provides the original client IP address to the origin web server. `True-Client-IP` is only available on an Enterprise plan. In the example below, `203.0.113.1` is the original visitor IP address. For example: `True-Client-IP: 203.0.113.1`\nL247: There is no difference between the `True-Client-IP` and `CF-Connecting-IP` headers besides the name of the header. Some Enterprise customers with legacy devices need `True-Client-IP` to avoid updating firewalls or load-balancers to read a custom header name.\nL248: \nL249: To add a `True-Client-IP` HTTP header to requests, cite139†enable the Add \"True-Client-IP\" header Managed Transform .\nL250: Alternatively, if you do not wish to receive the `True-Client-IP` header or any HTTP header that may contain the visitor's IP address, cite139†enable the Remove visitor IP headers Managed Transform .\nL251: \nL252: Caution\nL253: \nL254: If you are using Cloudflare in a stacked CDN and authenticating HTTP requests based on the IP address value in the `True-Client-IP` header, you must add a `True-Client-IP` header to your requests. If you do not add this header, its value can be spoofed to any value.\nL255: ### X-Forwarded-For\nL256: \nL257: `X-Forwarded-For` maintains proxy server and original visitor IP addresses. If there was no existing `X-Forwarded-For`header in the request sent to Cloudflare, `X-Forwarded-For` has an identical value to the `CF-Connecting-IP` header.\nL258: \nL259: For example, if the original visitor IP address is `203.0.113.1` and the request sent to Cloudflare does not contain an `X-Forwarded-For` header, then Cloudflare will send `X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.1` to the origin.\nL260: If, on the other hand, an `X-Forwarded-For` header was already present in the request to Cloudflare, Cloudflare will append the IP address of the HTTP proxy connecting to Cloudflare to the header.\nL261: For example, if the original visitor IP address is `203.0.113.1` and a request is proxied through two proxies: proxy A with an IP address of `198.51.100.101` and proxy B with an IP address of `198.51.100.102` before being proxied to Cloudflare, then Cloudflare will send `X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.1,198.51.100.101,198.51.100.102` to the origin.\nL262: Proxy A will append the original visitor's IP address (`203.0.113.1`) to `X-Forwarded-For` before proxying the request to proxy B which, in turn, will append Proxy A's IP address (`198.51.100.101`) to `X-Forwarded-For` before proxying the request to Cloudflare. And finally, Cloudflare will append proxy B's IP address (`198.51.100.102`) to `X-Forwarded-For` before proxying the request to the origin.\nL263: If you do not wish to receive the visitor's IP address (and other intermediate proxy IP addresses) in the `X-Forwarded-For` header, or any HTTP header that may contain the visitor's IP address, cite139†enable the Remove visitor IP headers Managed Transform . For the `X-Forwarded-For` header specifically, this Managed Transform will only remove the visitor IP from the header value when Cloudflare receives a request proxied by at least another CDN.\nL264: In this case, Cloudflare will only keep the IP address of the last proxy.\nL265: Using the previous example where a request was proxied twice (proxies A and B) before being proxied through Cloudflare, with Remove visitor IP headers enabled, Cloudflare would send `X-Forwarded-For: 198.51.100.102` to the origin, keeping only proxy B's IP address (the last proxy before Cloudflare). Refer to cite143†Visitor IP address in the `x-forwarded-for` HTTP header for more details.\nL266: \nL267: Note\nL268: To restore the original visitor IP address at your origin web server, Cloudflare recommends that your logs or applications look at `CF-Connecting-IP` or `True-Client-IP` instead of `X-Forwarded-For`. `CF-Connecting-IP` and `True-Client-IP` both have a consistent format containing only one IP address.\nL269: ### X-Forwarded-Proto\nL270: \nL271: `X-Forwarded-Proto` is used to identify the protocol (HTTP or HTTPS) that a visitor used to connect to Cloudflare. By default, the protocol used is `https`, unless the visitor selected a different cite144†encryption mode .\nL272: \nL273: For incoming requests, the value of this header will be set to the protocol the client used (`http` or `https`). If the client set a different value, it will be overwritten.\nL274: ### Cf-Ray\nL275: \nL276: The `Cf-Ray` header (otherwise known as a cite111†Ray ID ) is a hashed value that encodes information about the data center and the visitor's request. For example: `Cf-Ray: 230b030023ae2822-SJC`.\nL277: \nL278: The Cf-Ray header identifies the data center processing the request when displayed as a response header. This is represented by a three-letter code corresponding to the data center's location.\nL279: The Cf-Ray header is also sent to upstream origins and may be modified to reflect the connecting data center. This occurs when a request is routed through cite145†Argo Smart Routing or cite146†Argo Tiered Caching . In such cases, the three-letter code in the Cf-Ray header will indicate the data center connecting to the origin, not the ingress data center.\nL280: \nL281: Add the cite147†`Cf-Ray` header to your origin web server logs to match requests proxied to Cloudflare to requests in your server logs.\nL282: Enterprise customers can see all requests via cite148†Cloudflare Logs , including data related to the ingress data center.\nL283: ### CF-IPCountry\nL284: \nL285: The `CF-IPCountry` header contains a two-character country code of the originating visitor's country.\nL286: \nL287: Besides the cite149†ISO-3166-1 alpha-2 codes ↗︎†www.iso.org , Cloudflare uses the following special country codes:\nL288: \nL289:   * `XX` - Used for clients without country code data.\nL290:   * `T1` - Used for clients using the Tor network.\nL291: To add this header to requests, along with other HTTP headers with location information for the visitor's IP address, cite139†enable the Add visitor location headers Managed Transform .\nL292: \nL293: Note\nL294: \nL295: The `CF-IPCountry` header is removed from requests made from a Worker to an origin that is not proxied behind Cloudflare.\nL296: ### CF-Visitor\nL297: \nL298: Currently, this header is a JSON object, containing only one key called `scheme`. The header will be either HTTP or HTTPS, and it is only relevant if you need to enable Flexible SSL in your Cloudflare settings. For example: `CF-Visitor: { \\\"scheme\\\":\\\"https\\\"}`.\nL299: \nL300: ### CDN-Loop\nL301: \nL302: `CDN-Loop` allows Cloudflare to specify how many times a request can enter Cloudflare's network before it is blocked as a looping request. For example: `CDN-Loop: cloudflare`.\nL303: ### CF-Connecting-O2O\nL304: \nL305: If cite150†SSL for SaaS is used for cite151†the SaaS provider-owned zone , a HTTP header will be set to `cf-connecting-o2o: 1`.\nL306: ### CF-Worker\nL307: \nL308: The `CF-Worker` request header is added to an edge Worker subrequest that identifies the host that spawned the subrequest. For example: `CF-Worker: example.com`.\nL309: \nL310: You can add `CF-Worker` header on server logs similar to the way you add the cite147†`CF-RAY` header. To do that, add `$http_cf_worker` in the log format file: `log_format cf_custom \"CF-Worker:$http_cf_worker\"'`\nL311: `CF-Worker` is added to all Worker subrequests sent via `fetch()`. It is set to the name of the zone which owns the Worker making the subrequest. For example, a Worker script on route for `foo.example.com/*` from `example.com` will have all subrequests with the header:\nL312: \nL313:     `CF-Worker: example.com`\nL314: The intended purpose of this header is to provide a means for recipients (for example, origins, load balancers, other Workers) to recognize, filter, and route traffic generated by Workers on specific zones.\nL315: \nL316: Note\nL317: \nL318: When configuring WAF custom rules, do not match on this header. These rules are applied before Cloudflare adds the `CF-Worker` header. Instead, use the cite152†`cf.worker.upstream_zone` field, which contains the same value and exists for the same purpose.\nL319: To block a specific Worker, add a `Block` action triggered by the expression `cf.worker.upstream_zone eq \"example.com\"`.\nL320: \nL321: To block all Worker subrequests except those from your own zone's Worker, add a `Block` action triggered by the expression `not (cf.worker.upstream_zone in {\"\" \"customer-zone.com\"})`.\nL322: ### Connection\nL323: \nL324: For incoming requests, the value of this header will always be set to `Keep-Alive`. If the client set a different value, such as `close`, it will be overwritten. Note that is also the case when the client uses HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 to connect.\nL325: ### Considerations for Spectrum\nL326: \nL327: When using Spectrum with a TCP application, these headers are not visible at the origin as they are HTTP headers. If you wish to utilize these in your application, there are two options:\nL328: \nL329:   * Use an HTTP or HTTPS Spectrum app instead of TCP\nL330:   * Use the cite153†Proxy Protocol feature L331: \nL332: ## Response headers\nL333: \nL334: Cloudflare will remove some HTTP headers from the response sent back to the visitor and add some Cloudflare-specific HTTP headers.\nL335: ### Removed response headers\nL336: \nL337: Cloudflare passes all HTTP headers in the response from the origin server back to the visitor with the exception of the following headers:\nL338: \nL339:   * `X-Accel-Buffering`\nL340:   * `X-Accel-Charset`\nL341:   * `X-Accel-Limit-Rate`\nL342:   * `X-Accel-Redirect`\nL343:   * `Alt-Svc`\nL344: \nL345: ### Added response headers\nL346: \nL347: Cloudflare adds the HTTP headers specified below to the response sent to the visitor.\nL348: #### Cf-Ray\nL349: \nL350: The `Cf-Ray` value returned to the visitor will be the same `Cf-Ray` value that was sent to the origin server.\nL351: \nL352: #### Cf-Cache-Status\nL353: \nL354: A list of all possible `Cf-Cache-Status` values is contained in cite154†Cloudflare cache responses .\nL355: \nL356: cite109†PreviousCloudflare crawlers cite111†NextCloudflare Ray ID L357: \nL358: Was this helpful?\nL359: \nL360: Yes No\nL361: \nL362: cite155†Edit page†github.com cite156†Report issue†github.com L363: ## On this page\nL364:   * cite157†Overview L365:   * cite158†Request headers L366:   * cite159†Accept-Encoding L367:   * cite160†CF-Connecting-IP L368:   * cite161†CF-Connecting-IPv6 L369:   * cite162†CF-EW-Via L370:   * cite163†CF-Pseudo-IPv4 L371:   * cite164†True-Client-IP (Enterprise plan only) L372:   * cite165†X-Forwarded-For L373:   * cite166†X-Forwarded-Proto L374:   * cite167†Cf-Ray L375:   * cite168†CF-IPCountry L376:   * cite169†CF-Visitor L377:   * cite170†CDN-Loop L378:   * cite171†CF-Connecting-O2O L379:   * cite172†CF-Worker L380:   * cite173†Connection L381:   * cite174†Considerations for Spectrum L382:   * cite175†Response headers L383:   * cite176†Removed response headers L384:   * cite177†Added response headers L385: cite155†Edit page†github.com cite156†Report issue†github.com L386: \nL387: Getting started\nL388: \nL389: cite178†Plans†www.cloudflare.com cite179†Contact sales†www.cloudflare.com cite180†Partners†www.cloudflare.com cite181†Find a partner†partnerlocator.cloudflare.com cite182†Startups†www.cloudflare.com cite183†Under attack?†www.cloudflare.com cite184†Domain name search†domains.cloudflare.com L390: \nL391: Company\nL392: cite185†About†www.cloudflare.com cite186†Careers†www.cloudflare.com cite187†Investors†cloudflare.net cite188†Press†www.cloudflare.com cite189†Press kit†www.cloudflare.com cite190†Global network†www.cloudflare.com L393: \nL394: Public interest\nL395: \nL396: cite191†Project Galileo†www.cloudflare.com cite192†Athenian Project†www.cloudflare.com cite193†Cloudflare for Campaigns†www.cloudflare.com cite194†Project Fairshot†www.cloudflare.com cite195†Impact/ESG†www.cloudflare.com L397: \nL398: Compliance\nL399: cite196†Compliance resources†www.cloudflare.com cite197†Trust Hub†www.cloudflare.com cite198†Data Protection†www.cloudflare.com cite199†Responsible AI†www.cloudflare.com cite200†Transparency report†www.cloudflare.com cite98†Report abuse†www.cloudflare.com L400: \nL401: Resources\nL402: cite201†App innovation report†www.cloudflare.com cite202†Cloudflare Radar†radar.cloudflare.com cite203†Case studies†www.cloudflare.com cite204†Status†www.cloudflarestatus.com cite205†Support†support.cloudflare.com cite206†Events†www.cloudflare.com cite207†Blog†blog.cloudflare.com L403: \nL404: Developers\nL405: \nL406: cite135†Documentation cite208†Learning center†www.cloudflare.com cite209†Community†community.cloudflare.com L407: \nL408: Solutions\nL409: cite210†SSE and SASE platform†www.cloudflare.com cite211†Cloudflare AI Cloud†www.cloudflare.com cite212†AI Security†www.cloudflare.com cite213†Frontend Development Platform†www.cloudflare.com cite214†Multi-Tenant Platform Development†www.cloudflare.com cite215†Web Security Platform†www.cloudflare.com L410: \nL411: cite216†Start Building†dash.cloudflare.com cite217†Log In†dash.cloudflare.com L412: \nL413: © 2026 Cloudflare, Inc.\nL414: cite218†Privacy policy†www.cloudflare.com | cite219†Report security issues†www.cloudflare.com | cite220†Terms of use†www.cloudflare.com | cite221†Trademark†www.cloudflare.com L415: \nL416: cite222†Image: privacy options [Button: Your privacy choices]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRequest · Cloudflare Workers docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/request/)\nciteturn23view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view1\",\"pattern\":\"upstream_zone\"}); Total lines: 1072\nNo matching text found for \"upstream_zone\""},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"5fbb67\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000007709,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":157,\"output\":\"/**\\n * Runtime bridge factories and RPC helpers. This entry must not depend on\\n * resource providers or local development tooling, even through dynamic imports.\\n */\\nexport * from \\\"./Fetcher.ts\\\";\\nexport * from \\\"./Workers/InferEnv.ts\\\";\\nexport * from \\\"./Workers/Rpc.ts\\\";\\nexport * from \\\"./Workers/RpcAsync.ts\\\";\\n\\n// ── runtime bridge factories ──\\nexport { makeDurableObjectBridge } from \\\"./Workers/DurableObjectBridge.ts\\\";\\nexport { makeWorkerBridge } from \\\"./Workers/WorkerBridge.ts\\\";\\nexport { withSqlMigrations } from \\\"./Workers/SqlMigrationsRuntime.ts\\\";\\nexport { makeWorkflowBridge } from \\\"./Workflows/WorkflowBridge.ts\\\";\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a10e2a-6616-7a42-8b17-50e4af22487f","create_time":1791262258.061473}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
