{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T04:51:22.690Z","ordinal":37888,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a10e2a-6616-7a42-8b17-50e4af22487f","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-3d119184-38c6-4c3f-a40c-d90301e12c7f","query":"site:developers.cloudflare.com CF-Worker spoof incoming header worker subrequests rate limit ...","action":{"type":"search","query":null,"queries":["site:developers.cloudflare.com CF-Worker spoof incoming header worker subrequests rate limit","site:developers.cloudflare.com request cf worker subrequest same zone identity client IP"]},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search0","snippet":"To prevent this behavior, you must exclude any Workers subrequests coming from the same zone from your rate limiting rule using the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field. ...","title":"Troubleshoot rate limiting rules · Cloudflare Web Application Firewall (WAF) docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/waf/rate-limiting-rules/troubleshooting/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search1","snippet":"To restore the original visitor IP address at your origin web server, Cloudflare recommends that your logs or applications look at `CF-Connecting-IP` or `True-Client-IP` instead","title":"Cloudflare HTTP headers · Cloudflare Fundamentals docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/reference/http-headers/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search2","snippet":"The `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field identifies the zone that spawned a Workers subrequest. ... Use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field instead, which holds the same value.","title":"Block Worker subrequests from other zones · Cloudflare Web Application Firewall (WAF) docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/waf/custom-rules/use-cases/block-worker-subrequests/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search3","snippet":"You can define and configure multiple rate limiting configurations per Worker, which allows you to define different limits against incoming request and/or user parameters as","title":"Rate Limiting · Cloudflare Workers docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/rate-limit/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search4","snippet":"When a request hits a zone with a Worker, the initial HTTP request log represents the end-user request to the Worker. ... Because the initial","title":"Worker subrequests · Cloudflare Logs docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/logs/faq/worker-subrequests/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"9a51ac8b.previews.developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search5","snippet":"The following request header transform rule adds an HTTP header to Workers subrequests coming from a different zone: ... The `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field used in the","title":"Add a request header for subrequests from other zones · Cloudflare Rules docs","url":"https://9a51ac8b.previews.developers.cloudflare.com/rules/transform/examples/add-request-header-subrequest-other-zone/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search6","snippet":"You can now use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field in Transform Rules to control rule execution based on whether a request originates from Workers, including subrequests issued","title":"Match Workers subrequests by upstream zone — cf.worker.upstream_zone now supported in Transform Rules · Changelog","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/changelog/post/2025-06-09-transform-rule-subrequest-matching/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search7","snippet":"If `worker-a` sends a fetch request to `worker-b`, the request will fail, because of the limitation on same-zone fetch requests. `worker-a` must have a service","title":"Custom Domains · Cloudflare Workers docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/routing/custom-domains/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search8","snippet":"With a no-op Worker (a Worker that simply proxies traffic by passing on the original client request to the origin and proxying the response) running","title":"Cloudflare analytics with Workers · Cloudflare Analytics docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/analytics/account-and-zone-analytics/analytics-with-workers/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search9","snippet":"All HTTP request and response headers are available through the Headers API ↗︎. ... These differences are intentional, and reflect the server-side nature of the","title":"Headers · Cloudflare Workers docs","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/headers/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"d1-custom-bindings-docs.previews.developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search10","snippet":"Workers scale automatically across the Cloudflare global network. ... Accounts on the Workers Free plan have a daily request limit of 100,000 requests, resetting at","title":"Limits · Cloudflare Workers docs","url":"https://d1-custom-bindings-docs.previews.developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"9a798b9b.previews.developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search11","snippet":"The following request header transform rule adds an HTTP header to Workers subrequests coming from a different zone: ... The `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field used in the","title":"Add a request header for subrequests from other zones · Cloudflare Rules docs","url":"https://9a798b9b.previews.developers.cloudflare.com/rules/transform/examples/add-request-header-subrequest-other-zone/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.cloudflare.com","ref_id":"turn24search12","snippet":"Cloudflare looks at various aspects of a request, including the source IP, the requested domain, and the identity of the authenticated user initiating the request.","title":"Cloudflare | Evolving to a SASE architecture with Cloudflare 44","url":"https://developers.cloudflare.com/reference-architecture/static/cloudflare-evolving-to-a-sase-architecture.pdf"}]},"started_at_ms":1791262279775,"completed_at_ms":1791262282690}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T04:51:22.976Z","ordinal":37889,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a10e2a-6616-7a42-8b17-50e4af22487f","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-c4455b5a-8b2d-4ffb-9f26-f191d97cd9a7","process_id":"34451","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n 'Constructor|callback|headers|fromWeb' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/WorkerBridge.ts | head -25; rg --files -uu node_modules | rg 'workers-types.*/index.d.ts$' | head -4; cat docs/architecture/frontend.md | head -50"],"cwd":"file:///Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'Constructor|callback|headers|fromWeb' node_modules/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/WorkerBridge.ts","query":"Constructor|callback|headers|fromWeb","path":"WorkerBridge.ts"},{"type":"list_files","cmd":"rg --files -uu node_modules","path":"node_modules"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg 'workers-types.*/index.d.ts$'","query":"workers-types.*/index.d.ts$","path":null},{"type":"read","cmd":"cat docs/architecture/frontend.md","name":"frontend.md","path":"docs/architecture/frontend.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"node_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/index.d.ts\nnode_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/experimental/index.d.ts\n---\ndocument_type: architecture\nlifecycle: current\nauthority: canonical\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-05\nreview_trigger: frontend runtime, transport, rendering, build adapter, or composition change\n---\n\n# Frontend\n\nTaxKit has two browser-facing TanStack Start apps: `apps/web` is the native\ncalculator Website candidate and `apps/docs` remains the public developer\ndocumentation app until its replacement is qualified.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis doc covers browser/runtime boundaries and frontend ownership. It does not\ndefine tax calculation rules.\n\n## Main areas\n\n`apps/web`\n: Native Website candidate with checked settings, a take-home form, server\n  rendering over the private API binding and direct browser RPC.\n\n`apps/api`\n: Retained standalone Bun API and native Alchemy Worker candidate. The Worker\n  serves HTTP and RPC through the same checked calculator operation.\n\n`apps/docs`\n: Fumadocs-backed public documentation site for rule references, API docs, SDK\n  guides and contributor docs. It owns the route runtime, app shell, app-local\n  MDX component map and browser rendering. Route files import that shared map;\n  they do not define local `mdxComponents` objects or inline MDX registries.\n  Server loaders consume\n  `@taxkit/docs-content`; browser modules consume browser-safe\n  `@taxkit/docs-content/client`, `@taxkit/docs-content/schemas` and\n  `@taxkit/docs-fumadocs/render` exports.\n\n`packages/ui`\n: Planned shared UI primitives for TaxKit-owned apps, once repeated UI\npatterns justify a package.\n\n## Runtime shape\n\nBrowser code should consume browser-safe client/schema exports and reach the\nAPI through the configured API base URL. Server-only handlers, filesystem code\nand Node adapters must stay behind explicit server exports and out of\n`apps/web`.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"node_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/index.d.ts\nnode_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/experimental/index.d.ts\n---\ndocument_type: architecture\nlifecycle: current\nauthority: canonical\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-05\nreview_trigger: frontend runtime, transport, rendering, build adapter, or composition change\n---\n\n# Frontend\n\nTaxKit has two browser-facing TanStack Start apps: `apps/web` is the native\ncalculator Website candidate and `apps/docs` remains the public developer\ndocumentation app until its replacement is qualified.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis doc covers browser/runtime boundaries and frontend ownership. It does not\ndefine tax calculation rules.\n\n## Main areas\n\n`apps/web`\n: Native Website candidate with checked settings, a take-home form, server\n  rendering over the private API binding and direct browser RPC.\n\n`apps/api`\n: Retained standalone Bun API and native Alchemy Worker candidate. The Worker\n  serves HTTP and RPC through the same checked calculator operation.\n\n`apps/docs`\n: Fumadocs-backed public documentation site for rule references, API docs, SDK\n  guides and contributor docs. It owns the route runtime, app shell, app-local\n  MDX component map and browser rendering. Route files import that shared map;\n  they do not define local `mdxComponents` objects or inline MDX registries.\n  Server loaders consume\n  `@taxkit/docs-content`; browser modules consume browser-safe\n  `@taxkit/docs-content/client`, `@taxkit/docs-content/schemas` and\n  `@taxkit/docs-fumadocs/render` exports.\n\n`packages/ui`\n: Planned shared UI primitives for TaxKit-owned apps, once repeated UI\npatterns justify a package.\n\n## Runtime shape\n\nBrowser code should consume browser-safe client/schema exports and reach the\nAPI through the configured API base URL. Server-only handlers, filesystem code\nand Node adapters must stay behind explicit server exports and out of\n`apps/web`.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":78477084},"formatted_output":"node_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/index.d.ts\nnode_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/experimental/index.d.ts\n---\ndocument_type: architecture\nlifecycle: current\nauthority: canonical\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-05\nreview_trigger: frontend runtime, transport, rendering, build adapter, or composition change\n---\n\n# Frontend\n\nTaxKit has two browser-facing TanStack Start apps: `apps/web` is the native\ncalculator Website candidate and `apps/docs` remains the public developer\ndocumentation app until its replacement is qualified.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis doc covers browser/runtime boundaries and frontend ownership. It does not\ndefine tax calculation rules.\n\n## Main areas\n\n`apps/web`\n: Native Website candidate with checked settings, a take-home form, server\n  rendering over the private API binding and direct browser RPC.\n\n`apps/api`\n: Retained standalone Bun API and native Alchemy Worker candidate. The Worker\n  serves HTTP and RPC through the same checked calculator operation.\n\n`apps/docs`\n: Fumadocs-backed public documentation site for rule references, API docs, SDK\n  guides and contributor docs. It owns the route runtime, app shell, app-local\n  MDX component map and browser rendering. Route files import that shared map;\n  they do not define local `mdxComponents` objects or inline MDX registries.\n  Server loaders consume\n  `@taxkit/docs-content`; browser modules consume browser-safe\n  `@taxkit/docs-content/client`, `@taxkit/docs-content/schemas` and\n  `@taxkit/docs-fumadocs/render` exports.\n\n`packages/ui`\n: Planned shared UI primitives for TaxKit-owned apps, once repeated UI\npatterns justify a package.\n\n## Runtime shape\n\nBrowser code should consume browser-safe client/schema exports and reach the\nAPI through the configured API base URL. Server-only handlers, filesystem code\nand Node adapters must stay behind explicit server exports and out of\n`apps/web`.\n"},"started_at_ms":1791262282897,"completed_at_ms":1791262282976}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T04:51:23.026Z","ordinal":37890,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a10f8d-5512-76d2-8e68-0c0a887d6a72","call_id":"call_bnyE9VRYpaNR5UjP4J07jUeF","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Troubleshoot rate limiting rules · Cloudflare Web Application Firewall (WAF) docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/waf/rate-limiting-rules/troubleshooting/)\nciteturn24search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; To prevent this behavior, you must exclude any Workers subrequests coming from the same zone from your rate limiting rule using the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field. ... If you use Origin Rules to rewrite the `Host` header and your rate limiting rule includes `http.host` in its expression or counting characteristics, the rule may match incoming requests but fail to increment its counter. ... As a result, the rule matches requests but never increments the counter, and the rate limit is never enforced.\n\n# Troubleshoot rate limiting rules\n\nLast updated May 5, 2026|Copy as Markdown|View as Markdown|Agent setup\n\n[Select]\n\n## Some Workers subrequests are counted as separate requests\n\nCloudflare may count Workers subrequests on the same zone as separate requests, which will cause a rate limiting rule to trigger sooner than expected. This behavior happens when the rate limiting rule is configured with Also apply rate limiting to cached assets set to false.\n\nTo prevent this behavior, you must exclude any Workers subrequests coming from the same zone from your rate limiting rule using the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field. For example, you could add the following sub-expression to your rate limiting rule expression:\n    \n    `and (cf.worker.upstream_zone == \"\" or cf.worker.upstream_zone != \"<YOUR_ZONE>\")`\n\nThe first condition (testing for an empty string) will match direct visitor requests, while the second condition will match subrequests not originating from your zone, effectively excluding subrequests from the same zone from the rate limiting rule.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCloudflare HTTP headers · Cloudflare Fundamentals docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/reference/http-headers/)\nciteturn24search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; To restore the original visitor IP address at your origin web server, Cloudflare recommends that your logs or applications look at `CF-Connecting-IP` or `True-Client-IP` instead of `X-Forwarded-For`. ... The `CF-Worker` request header is added to an edge Worker subrequest that identifies the host that spawned the subrequest. ... Instead, use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field, which contains the same value and exists for the same purpose.\n### CDN-Loop\n\n`CDN-Loop` allows Cloudflare to specify how many times a request can enter Cloudflare's network before it is blocked as a looping request. For example: `CDN-Loop: cloudflare`.\n\n### CF-Connecting-O2O\n\nIf SSL for SaaS is used for the SaaS provider-owned zone, a HTTP header will be set to `cf-connecting-o2o: 1`.\n\n### CF-Worker\n\nThe `CF-Worker` request header is added to an edge Worker subrequest that identifies the host that spawned the subrequest. For example: `CF-Worker: example.com`.\n\nYou can add `CF-Worker` header on server logs similar to the way you add the `CF-RAY` header. To do that, add `$http_cf_worker` in the log format file: `log_format cf_custom \"CF-Worker:$http_cf_worker\"'`\n\n`CF-Worker` is added to all Worker subrequests sent via `fetch()`. It is set to the name of the zone which owns the Worker making the subrequest. For example, a Worker script on route for `foo.example.com/*` from `example.com` will have all subrequests with the header:\n    \n    `CF-Worker: example.com`\n\nThe intended purpose of this header is to provide a means for recipients (for example, origins, load balancers, other Workers) to recognize, filter, and route traffic generated by Workers on specific zones.\n\nNote\n\nWhen configuring WAF custom rules, do not match on this header. These rules are applied before Cloudflare adds the `CF-Worker` header. Instead, use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field, which contains the same value and exists for the same purpose.\n\nTo block a specific Worker, add a `Block` action triggered by the expression `cf.worker.upstream_zone eq \"example.com\"`.\n\nTo block all Worker subrequests except those from your own zone's Worker, add a `Block` action triggered by the expression `not (cf.worker.upstream_zone in {\"\" \"customer-zone.com\"})`.\n\n### Connection\n\nFor incoming requests, the value of this header will always be set to `Keep-Alive`. If the client set a different value, such as `close`, it will be overwritten. Note that is also the case when the client uses HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 to connect.\n\n### Considerations for Spectrum\n\nWhen using Spectrum with a TCP application, these headers are not visible at the origin as they are HTTP headers. If you wish to utilize these in your application, there are two options:\n\n  * Use an HTTP or HTTPS Spectrum app instead of TCP\n  * Use the Proxy Protocol feature\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBlock Worker subrequests from other zones · Cloudflare Web Application Firewall (WAF) docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/waf/custom-rules/use-cases/block-worker-subrequests/)\nciteturn24search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: yesterday; Crawled: today; The `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field identifies the zone that spawned a Workers subrequest. ... Use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field instead, which holds the same value.\n\n# Block Worker subrequests from other zones\n\nLast updated Oct 5, 2026|Copy as Markdown|View as Markdown|Agent setup\n\n[Select]\n\nThe `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field identifies the zone that spawned a Workers subrequest. You can use this field in custom rules to mitigate unwanted Worker traffic.\n\nCaution\n\nDo not use the `CF-Worker` request header in WAF rules. The header is added after rule evaluation, so it is not available when WAF rules run. Use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field instead, which holds the same value.\n\n### Block subrequests from a specific zone\n\n  * When incoming requests match:\n\nIf you are using the expression editor:\n`(cf.worker.upstream_zone eq \"example.com\")`\n\n  * Then take action: Block\n\n### Block all Worker subrequests except from your own zone\n\n  * When incoming requests match:\n\nIf you are using the expression editor:\n`(not cf.worker.upstream_zone in {\"\" \"your-zone.com\"})`\n\n  * Then take action: Block\n\nThe empty string matches requests that did not come from a Worker, so this expression only blocks subrequests from other zones. Direct visitor traffic is not affected.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRate Limiting · Cloudflare Workers docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/rate-limit/)\nciteturn24search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; You can define and configure multiple rate limiting configurations per Worker, which allows you to define different limits against incoming request and/or user parameters as needed to protect your application or upstream APIs. ... For example, if many requests come in to your Worker in a single Cloudflare location, all rate limited on the same key, the isolate that serves each request will check against its locally cached value of the rate limit. ...   * Workers Analytics Engine — Add an Analytics Engine binding to your Worker and emit custom data points (for example, a `rate_limited` event) when `limit()` returns `{ success: false }`. ...   * `hono-cf-rate-limit` ↗︎ — Middleware for Hono applications that applies rate limiting in Cloudflare Workers, powered by Wrangler’s built-in features.\n\nRuntime APIs\n\n  4. /Bindings (env)\n  5. /Rate Limiting\n\n# Rate Limiting\n\nLast updated Apr 23, 2026|Copy as Markdown|View as Markdown|Agent setup\n\n[Select]\n\nThe Rate Limiting API lets you define rate limits and write code around them in your Worker.\n\nYou can use it to enforce:\n\n  * Rate limits that are applied after your Worker starts, only once a specific part of your code is reached\n  * Different rate limits for different types of customers or users (ex: free vs. paid)\n  * Resource-specific or path-specific limits (ex: limit per API route)\n  * Any combination of the above\n\nThe Rate Limiting API is backed by the same infrastructure that serves rate limiting rules.\n\nNote\n\nYou must use version 4.36.0 or later of the Wrangler CLI.\n\n## Get started\n\nFirst, add a binding to your Worker that gives it access to the Rate Limiting API:\n    \n    `{\n    \t\"main\": \"src/index.js\",\n    \t\"ratelimits\": [\n    \t\t{\n    \t\t\t\"name\": \"MY_RATE_LIMITER\",\n    \t\t\t// An identifier you define, that is unique to your Cloudflare account.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWorker subrequests · Cloudflare Logs docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/logs/faq/worker-subrequests/)\nciteturn24search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; When a request hits a zone with a Worker, the initial HTTP request log represents the end-user request to the Worker. ... Because the initial log entry only covers the request from the client to the Worker, `OriginResponseStatus` is `0` and `OriginIP` is empty on that entry. ... Using the initial request together with the matching subrequest entries lets you reconstruct the full request path from client to Worker to origin.\n\n# Worker subrequests\n\nLast updated Apr 23, 2026|Copy as Markdown|View as Markdown|Agent setup\n\n### Why are the origin fields empty on the initial request when a Worker makes a subrequest?\n\nWhen a request hits a zone with a Worker, the initial HTTP request log represents the end-user request to the Worker. If that Worker later makes a `fetch()` request to your origin, Cloudflare writes a second HTTP request log for that Worker subrequest.\n\nBecause the initial log entry only covers the request from the client to the Worker, `OriginResponseStatus` is `0` and `OriginIP` is empty on that entry. Those fields are populated on the second log entry, which represents the Worker-to-origin fetch. For more on what `OriginResponseStatus=0` means in different contexts, refer to 504 responses with origin status 0 in Logpush.\n\n### What the two log entries represent\n\nLog entry  | Typical `ClientRequestSource` value  | What it represents  | Origin fields\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nInitial request  | `eyeball`  | End-user request to the Worker  | `OriginResponseStatus` is `0`, `OriginIP` is empty\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdd a request header for subrequests from other zones · Cloudflare Rules docs (https://9a51ac8b.previews.developers.cloudflare.com/rules/transform/examples/add-request-header-subrequest-other-zone/)\nciteturn24search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; The following request header transform rule adds an HTTP header to Workers subrequests coming from a different zone: ... The `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field used in the rule expression is set to empty if the current request is not a Workers subrequest.\n\n# Add a request header for subrequests from other zones\n\nCreate a request header transform rule to add an HTTP header when the Workers subrequest comes from a different zone.\n\nLast updated Aug 24, 2026|Copy as Markdown|View as Markdown|Agent setup\n\nThe following request header transform rule adds an HTTP header to Workers subrequests coming from a different zone:\n\nText in Expression Editor (replace `myappexample.com` with your domain):\n    \n    `(cf.worker.upstream_zone != \"\" and cf.worker.upstream_zone != \"myappexample.com\")`\n\nSelected operation under Modify request header: Set static\n\nHeader name: `X-External-Workers-Subrequest`\n\nValue: `1`\n\nThe `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field used in the rule expression is set to empty if the current request is not a Workers subrequest.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatch Workers subrequests by upstream zone — cf.worker.upstream_zone now supported in Transform Rules · Changelog (https://developers.cloudflare.com/changelog/post/2025-06-09-transform-rule-subrequest-matching/)\nciteturn24search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; You can now use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field in Transform Rules to control rule execution based on whether a request originates from Workers, including subrequests issued by Workers in other zones.\n\nBack to all postsJune 9, 2025\n\n## Match Workers subrequests by upstream zone — cf.worker.upstream_zone now supported in Transform Rules\n\nRules\n\nYou can now use the `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field in Transform Rules to control rule execution based on whether a request originates from Workers, including subrequests issued by Workers in other zones.\n\nImage: Match Workers subrequests by upstream zone in Transform Rules\n\nWhat's new:\n\n  * `cf.worker.upstream_zone` is now supported in Transform Rules expressions.\n  * Skip or apply logic conditionally when handling Workers subrequests.\n\nFor example, to add a header when the subrequest comes from another zone:\n\nText in Expression Editor (replace `myappexample.com` with your domain):\n    \n    `(cf.worker.upstream_zone != \"\" and cf.worker.upstream_zone != \"myappexample.com\")`\n\nSelected operation under Modify request header: Set static\n\nHeader name: `X-External-Workers-Subrequest`\n\nValue: `1`\n\nThis gives you more granular control in how you handle incoming requests for your zone.\n\nLearn more in the Transform Rules documentation and Rules language fields reference.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCustom Domains · Cloudflare Workers docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/routing/custom-domains/)\nciteturn24search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; If `worker-a` sends a fetch request to `worker-b`, the request will fail, because of the limitation on same-zone fetch requests. `worker-a` must have a service binding to `worker-b` for this request to resolve. ... For example, if you create a Custom Domain on `api.example.com` attached to your `api-gateway` Worker, a request to either `api.example.com/login` or `api.example.com/user` would invoke the same `api-gateway` Worker.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCloudflare analytics with Workers · Cloudflare Analytics docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/analytics/account-and-zone-analytics/analytics-with-workers/)\nciteturn24search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: today; With a no-op Worker (a Worker that simply proxies traffic by passing on the original client request to the origin and proxying the response) running on a particular route, the request to the origin is counted as a 'subrequest', separate from initial client to edge request. ...   * If your Worker sends a static response back to the client without ever calling fetch() to an origin, you are not making any subrequests, thus, all traffic will be shown in zone Analytics\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHeaders · Cloudflare Workers docs (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/headers/)\nciteturn24search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; All HTTP request and response headers are available through the Headers API ↗︎. ... These differences are intentional, and reflect the server-side nature of the Workers runtime. ... Despite the fact that the `Headers.getAll` method has been made obsolete in web browsers, Workers still provides this method for use with the `Set-Cookie` header. ... Any attempts to use `Headers.getAll` with other header names will throw an error. ... Cloudflare sets a number of its own custom headers on incoming requests and outgoing responses.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLimits · Cloudflare Workers docs (https://d1-custom-bindings-docs.previews.developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/)\nciteturn24search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Workers scale automatically across the Cloudflare global network. ... Accounts on the Workers Free plan have a daily request limit of 100,000 requests, resetting at midnight UTC. ... A subrequest is any request a Worker makes using the Fetch API or to Cloudflare services like R2, KV, or D1. ... You can change the subrequest limit per Worker using the `limits` configuration in your Wrangler configuration file.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdd a request header for subrequests from other zones · Cloudflare Rules docs (https://9a798b9b.previews.developers.cloudflare.com/rules/transform/examples/add-request-header-subrequest-other-zone/)\nciteturn24search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; The following request header transform rule adds an HTTP header to Workers subrequests coming from a different zone: ... The `cf.worker.upstream_zone` field used in the rule expression is set to empty if the current request is not a Workers subrequest.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCloudflare | Evolving to a SASE architecture with Cloudflare 44 (https://developers.cloudflare.com/reference-architecture/static/cloudflare-evolving-to-a-sase-architecture.pdf)\nciteturn24search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Cloudflare looks at various aspects of a request, including the source IP, the requested domain, and the identity of the authenticated user initiating the request.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"a035d8\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.07848775,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":504,\"output\":\"node_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/index.d.ts\\nnode_modules/.bun/@cloudflare+workers-types@5.20261003.1/node_modules/@cloudflare/workers-types/experimental/index.d.ts\\n---\\ndocument_type: architecture\\nlifecycle: current\\nauthority: canonical\\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-05\\nreview_trigger: frontend runtime, transport, rendering, build adapter, or composition change\\n---\\n\\n# Frontend\\n\\nTaxKit has two browser-facing TanStack Start apps: `apps/web` is the native\\ncalculator Website candidate and `apps/docs` remains the public developer\\ndocumentation app until its replacement is qualified.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nThis doc covers browser/runtime boundaries and frontend ownership. It does not\\ndefine tax calculation rules.\\n\\n## Main areas\\n\\n`apps/web`\\n: Native Website candidate with checked settings, a take-home form, server\\n  rendering over the private API binding and direct browser RPC.\\n\\n`apps/api`\\n: Retained standalone Bun API and native Alchemy Worker candidate. The Worker\\n  serves HTTP and RPC through the same checked calculator operation.\\n\\n`apps/docs`\\n: Fumadocs-backed public documentation site for rule references, API docs, SDK\\n  guides and contributor docs. It owns the route runtime, app shell, app-local\\n  MDX component map and browser rendering. Route files import that shared map;\\n  they do not define local `mdxComponents` objects or inline MDX registries.\\n  Server loaders consume\\n  `@taxkit/docs-content`; browser modules consume browser-safe\\n  `@taxkit/docs-content/client`, `@taxkit/docs-content/schemas` and\\n  `@taxkit/docs-fumadocs/render` exports.\\n\\n`packages/ui`\\n: Planned shared UI primitives for TaxKit-owned apps, once repeated UI\\npatterns justify a package.\\n\\n## Runtime shape\\n\\nBrowser code should consume browser-safe client/schema exports and reach the\\nAPI through the configured API base URL. Server-only handlers, filesystem code\\nand Node adapters must stay behind explicit server exports and out of\\n`apps/web`.\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a10e2a-6616-7a42-8b17-50e4af22487f","create_time":1791262283.026754}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T04:51:23.027Z","ordinal":37891,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"token_count","info":{"total_token_usage":{"input_tokens":716989636,"cached_input_tokens":702433024,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":3066840,"reasoning_output_tokens":1452484,"total_tokens":720056476},"last_token_usage":{"input_tokens":128154,"cached_input_tokens":122752,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":641,"reasoning_output_tokens":491,"total_tokens":128795},"model_context_window":258400},"rate_limits":{"limit_id":"codex","limit_name":null,"primary":{"used_percent":69.0,"window_minutes":10080,"resets_at":1791592581},"secondary":null,"credits":{"has_credits":true,"unlimited":false,"balance":"63000"},"individual_limit":null,"spend_control_reached":null,"plan_type":"pro","rate_limit_reached_type":null}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T04:51:42.736Z","ordinal":37892,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a10e2a-6616-7a42-8b17-50e4af22487f","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_02692a5b008ee3bd016ac47e4f120087d08c3a6e2a54384e79","summary_text":[],"raw_content":[]},"started_at_ms":1791262287107,"completed_at_ms":1791262302736}}
