{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T05:59:48.771Z","ordinal":39132,"type":"compacted","payload":{"compaction_response_id":"resp_02692a5b008ee3bd016ac48db05f0487d08a0b7b2ccfd8eb42","first_window_id":"01a1055e-4562-7d92-85e1-227f10cd2851","latest_token_usage_record":{"response_id":"resp_02692a5b008ee3bd016ac48db05f0487d08a0b7b2ccfd8eb42","root_turn_id":"01a10fab-f789-7f30-a7de-963ccee8ae9f","session_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","thread_token_usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":741818240,"input_tokens":757369752,"output_tokens":3563365,"reasoning_output_tokens":1500653,"total_tokens":760933117},"turn_id":"01a10fab-f789-7f30-a7de-963ccee8ae9f","turn_token_usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":12781952,"input_tokens":13166579,"output_tokens":58361,"reasoning_output_tokens":18234,"total_tokens":13224940},"usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":243712,"input_tokens":244886,"output_tokens":6642,"reasoning_output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":251528}},"message":"","previous_window_id":"01a10fb0-0c38-70b0-8b54-9d12f2e5c7a7","replacement_history":[{"content":[{"text":"\n# Files pasted by the user:\n\n## \"--- document_type: execution-plan lifecycle: current authority: supporting owne…\": /Users/cooper/.codex/attachments/f82088a9-b579-462a-9bee-4d3685805660/Pasted text.txt\n\nPasted text contains the user's request.\n\n## My request:\n\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1055e-500d-7de0-b833-96db813b1de3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791091429.389726,"turn_id":"01a1055e-488d-75e2-ab79-097882eda37b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a106da-6a9d-7e63-a676-d613ca83284b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1791116339.869503,"turn_id":"01a1055e-488d-75e2-ab79-097882eda37b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003capp-context\u003e\n# Codex desktop context\n- You are running inside the Codex (desktop) app, which allows some additional features not available in the CLI alone:\n\n### Images/Visuals/Files\n- In the app, the model can display images, videos, and audio using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When an app or connector generates or edits media, prefer native media already displayed inline or a local output file already returned by the tool. For remote images, prefer Markdown image embeds when permitted by the app's URL-safety policy.\n- For media that cannot be displayed directly, including remote video and audio, use the app's preview or display tool when available. Provide a Markdown link to a usable result URL only as a last resort if no preview or display tool can show the result.\n- Do not download remote media to work around display restrictions.\n- When sending or referencing a local image, video, or audio file, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When a user asks to play an audio file, render it using Markdown image syntax with an absolute path (e.g., ![audio](/absolute/path.mp3)).\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Pull request diff links\nWhen referencing code from a GitHub PR, you can link directly to its diff in the app using:\n[label](codex://review?pr=PR_URL\u0026path=FILE_PATH\u0026line=LINE\u0026side=right)\nURL-encode PR_URL and the repository-relative FILE_PATH. Use a verified one-based LINE from the current PR diff. Use side=left for the original code or side=right for the updated code. Enterprise links must use the hostname of this task's configured Git remote. Use ordinary file links for workspace code.\n\n### Workspace Dependencies\n- For sheets, slides, and documents, use the MCP server's `load_workspace_dependencies` tool (`mcp__codex_app__load_workspace_dependencies`) to find the bundled runtime and libraries.\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring automations, reminders, monitors, follow-ups, and thread wakeups. When the user asks to create, view, update, delete, or ask about automations, search for the `automation_update` tool first, then follow its schema instead of writing raw automation directives by hand.\n- For heartbeat monitors, preserve the user's notification intent in the saved prompt. Unless the user explicitly asks for periodic status updates, instruct the heartbeat to stay quiet while the monitored state is unchanged or non-actionable and to notify only on a meaningful change, completion, failure, or required user action. Do not add instructions such as \"leave a brief status update\" on every run.\n- When an automation should archive a Codex thread on completion, use `set_thread_archived` instead of emitting raw archive directives.\n\n### Thread Coordination\n- Treat the terms \"task\", \"thread\", \"chat\", and \"conversation\" as synonyms when they clearly refer to conversations in Codex. Use \"chat\" when referring to conversations in the product. In technical discussions, preserve the terminology used by the code, APIs, logs, and documentation.\n- When the user asks to create, fork, inspect, continue, hand off, pin, archive, unarchive, rename, or otherwise manage Codex threads, search for the relevant thread tool first: `create_thread`, `fork_thread`, `list_threads`, `list_archived_threads`, `read_thread`, `wait_threads`, `send_message_to_thread`, `handoff_thread`, `set_thread_pinned`, `set_thread_archived`, or `set_thread_title`.\n- When following another task's progress, prefer compact `wait_threads` snapshots over repeated `read_thread` calls. Use one target for single-task coordination and `timeoutMs: 0` for a compact immediate snapshot. `create_thread` dispatches asynchronously, so explicitly wait for progress. Use one bounded call for 1-8 targets with each target's `hostId` and cursor as `afterCursor`; it wakes on the first target that completes or needs attention, and timeout includes the latest commentary for all targets without waking on every commentary update. An up-to-date cursor suppresses already-delivered final text. Separate waits from one task may run serially. Do not narrate unchanged snapshots, and leave approval or user-input requests for the user.\n- Only use `create_thread` when the user explicitly asks to create a new thread. Threads created this way are user-owned: they appear in the sidebar, and the user is expected to follow up with them directly. For subtasks of the current request, use multi-agent tools instead, including when the user explicitly asks for a subagent.\n- After a successful `create_thread` call, emit `::created-thread{threadId=\"...\"}` for a created thread or `::created-thread{clientThreadId=\"...\"}` for queued worktree setup on its own line in your final response.\n\n### Inline Code Comments\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive when you need to attach feedback directly to specific code lines.\n- Emit one directive per inline comment; emit none when there are no actionable inline comments.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3).\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2}\n\n### Inline Artifact Follow-Ups\n- Format each artifact follow-up as an unescaped Markdown list item, `- :codex-followup[visible phrase]{prompt=\"Complete user request\"}`; avoid closing brackets in the visible phrase and escape double quotes in the prompt.\n\n### Git\n- Branch prefix: `codex/`. Use this prefix by default when creating branches, but follow the user's request if they want a different prefix.\n\u003c/app-context\u003e\n\nFor requests to create or edit a standalone LaTeX document, use the built-in editor by default. Create or edit the .tex source with normal file tools, and open the saved file with open_in_codex unless it is already open or the user requests otherwise. Keep follow-up edits in that same file and editor. Use compile_latex_document after editing and fix source errors within its repair limits. Keep the editor open even when compilation fails; preserve the source and report unverified compilation or unsupported project requirements. Discover these tools if deferred. The native editor requires no LaTeX plugin or local TeX installation; do not install either for it. Ordinary math explanations stay in chat.","type":"input_text"},{"text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -\u003e specific):\n\n- /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/skills/\u003cskill-name\u003e/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally \u003c= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `\u003cfile\u003e:\u003cline_start\u003e-\u003cline_end\u003e|note=[\u003chow memory was used\u003e]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `\u003crollout_ids\u003e` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `\u003ctimestamp\u003e-\u003cshort slug\u003e.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/cooper/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nCooper works across governed TypeScript/Effect infrastructure, Tilt Legal and HERO records/plugins, product sites, legal-search product design, career material, personal/wedding projects, and scheduled email workflows. They use macOS/zsh, Codex worktrees, Bun/pnpm, Cloudflare/Vercel, Executor and browser tools. They value Australian English, source-faithful claims, narrow authority, explicit proof ceilings, and practical delivery that preserves unrelated work.\n\n## User preferences\n\n- Treat “audit”, “report-only”, “planning-only”, “read only”, “Stop”, supplied IDs and named branches as hard boundaries; obtain exact authority immediately before external mutation.\n- For Alchemy/Effect implementation, refactoring, review or architecture, “always check the alchemy repo and effect repo for reference implementations and guidance”: inspect both relevant upstream repositories and the receiving project's installed versions/matching source/types before choosing an approach; upstream research never authorises an upgrade. [ad-hoc note]\n- Verify checkout/branch/IDs and requested end state. Separate local, CI, provider, deployment and user-visible proof; a green check or successful plan is not Production apply/proof.\n- Investigate the actual contract/root cause before concluding; label unknowns rather than inferring.\n- Treat visible billing/usage warnings as separate findings until they are tied to the exact failing workflow; prefer a narrow read-only diagnostic before changing settings or spending credits.\n- Preserve unrelated dirty work; inspect ignored/env files before cleanup. Never print or retain secret values.\n- Use concise factual Australian English and evidence-backed claims.\n- For job research, provide source material plus practical takeaways, match live roles to Cooper’s career materials, and verify Australia/Melbourne eligibility rather than trusting “Remote”.\n- For externally facing operational email, preserve technical distinctions, use clear/direct/warm wording, and leave drafts unsent unless sending is explicitly authorised.\n- For follow-up emails, compare against the live thread: keep the same warm practical invitation but do not repeat distinctive prior wording (\"I want it to sound like this but not be the same\").\n- When anonymising support correspondence, remove names/customer/project labels but retain identifiers the provider needs; state that identifiers are still identifying.\n- For mail triage, use only the required Executor surface, enumerate every `nextPageToken`, preserve bills/refunds/security/incident/wedding/travel/human-action mail, and give one numbered row per Inbox message. If output is too large, create a chunked/file-backed report—not a placeholder.\n- For credential mutation, modify the existing named object only when asked, preserve full policy/expiry, report permissions before/after, never expose its value, and independently read back identity and unchanged fields.\n- If a configured skill path is absent, load an equivalent only after checking its rules/references; a fallback skill never authorises substitute mail tooling.\n- For public profiles/docs, make only explicitly approved edits, keep sharing off unless requested, and verify rendered/saved result.\n- For identity/provider cutovers, produce a reviewable impact inventory first; preserve compatibility aliases and immutable IDs, and use narrow approved mutations with final provider/public readback.\n- For architecture/rebuild work, preserve stated content/design boundaries, research and settle decisions before implementation, and keep planning proof separate from runtime/provider/Production proof.\n- For visual brand audits, “Audit and tell me your proposed changes ELI5 with images” means visual, plain-language, proposal-first recommendations before edits; preserve old exploration in moodboards and confirm the exact approved logo/lockup before dependent changes.\n- For Linear release updates, state user impact, availability and required action, link engineering evidence, and retry uncertain writes by reading/reusing the existing record rather than creating duplicates.\n- For a requested Outlook draft, create and verify an unsent draft only; use a concise, non-technical handover unless detail is needed.\n- For client-impact analysis, separate confirmed configuration, residual uncertainty, test steps and a ready-to-send reply; when filing a requested thread, include the user’s related sent reply too.\n- When recovering client-work source material, trace repository copies back to their owning Outlook/Notion records; preserve originals and use verified SharePoint exports plus a durable source index rather than creating competing canonicals.\n- For naming, do not mechanically optimise for corporate/AI/agent wording, spelling or syllable count: preserve explicit favourites, imagery, sound and character; evaluate creative fit separately from current domain, trademark and business-name cleara…5980 tokens truncated… stops: 671 messages, 7 pages, `This action was rejected due to unacceptable risk`, `meMailFoldersUpdateMessagesContent`, `bodyBase64`\n  - desc: Latest exhaustive Gmail pagination/mixed-risk mutation stop and Outlook fail-closed read-state gate.\n  - learnings: Metadata batches of 10 and a complete chunked table precede mutation; never mix newsletters with Google security notices or use `bodyBase64` for `isRead`.\n\n### /Users/cooper/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-SharedLibraries-TILTLegal/Internal - Documents/Balance Legal Capital\n\n#### 2026-09-29\n\n- Common Costs source recovery and client filing: `feat/common-costs`, `docs/product-specs/common-costs/sources.md`, `Clients \u003e Balance Legal Capital \u003e Common costs`, `Common Costs/Correspondence`, PR #69\n  - desc: Canonical-source recovery from Mobius into Outlook/Notion, verified SharePoint exports, source index, and client routing records.\n  - learnings: `Balance Legal Capital/Common Costs` is the drive-root path; repository docs did not contain the original emails, so pivot to Outlook after checking the source list.\n\n### /Users/cooper/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-SharedLibraries-TILTLegal/Internal - Documents/HSF Kramer/HERO\n\n#### 2026-09-29\n\n- HERO WorkOS role-sync and approved Support request: Directory Sync, Directory Provisioning, `directory_managed`, `organization_membership.updated`, `isDraft: false`\n  - desc: WorkOS membership discrepancy traced upstream of Neon, plus name-free approved Support email and current-state query safeguards.\n  - learnings: Directory Provisioning is Support-enabled; inspect current membership API and verify Sent Items after explicit send authority.\n\n### /Users/cooper/Projects/tilt-legal-plugins\n\n#### 2026-09-29\n\n- Internal training, Foundations release, and knowledge-index boundary: Base Slide Deck, `Training/Internal`, `foundations:knowledge`, `agentic-ai-for-legal`, PR #68, `@tilt-legal/foundations@0.8.2`\n  - desc: Editable methodology deck, SharePoint/Notion filing, release verification, and read-only routing-versus-specialist-plugin architecture.\n  - learnings: “Internal” does not prove access; scope inherited permissions and separately read back CI, release, tag, `main`, and `stable`.\n\n### /Users/cooper/Projects/career\n\n#### 2026-09-29\n\n- Harvey interview and Australia-eligible role matching: Katie Burke, `_7k6pY6_YfM`, `exportYouTubeTranscript()`, `location=Remote`, Melbourne\n  - desc: Transcript fallback, hiring advice, and live-role eligibility/fit checks against Cooper’s career materials.\n  - learnings: Use YouTube captions when podcast retrieval fails; generic Remote did not establish Australian eligibility.\n\n### /Users/cooper/Documents/Codex/2026-09-29/hi-martina-that-s-not-great\n\n#### 2026-09-29\n\n- HERO Alkane offboarding wording: WorkOS deactivation, organisation deletion, live database, encrypted backups, 12 months\n  - desc: Careful external offboarding draft separating access, deletion and backup-retention decisions.\n  - learnings: Do not promise total immediate erasure; require written instructions and an execution time.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### /Users/cooper/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-TILTLegal/Codex/TILT Email Triage and /Users/cooper/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-coopercorbett@gmail.com/My Drive/Codex/Personal Emails\n\n- Outlook/Gmail triage mutation safety: `meMailFoldersUpdateMessagesContent`, `bodyBase64`, `This action was rejected due to unacceptable risk`, 350 unread, 632 messages\n  - desc: Older scheduled-triage connector safety and output-limit records; cwd-specific mailbox state is stale and must be re-queried.\n\n#### /Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/3ebe/taxkit and /Users/cooper/Projects/site, /Users/cooper/Projects/daw, /Users/cooper/Projects/bundjil, /Users/cooper/Projects/pollsor\n\n- Governed engineering and repository delivery: `runtime.server.ts`, `bun run verification`, Effect v4, Alchemy, Doppler, `Plan: no resources`\n  - desc: Search exact project name for infrastructure/release/deployment/cleanup; includes provider-proof boundaries, CI and architecture decisions.\n\n#### /Users/cooper/Projects/career and /Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/af47/career\n\n- Career, OpenAI application and LinkedIn: `evidence-bank.md`, `in:spam openai`, `Law Exchange Studies`, `Artificial Intelligence (AI)`\n  - desc: Source-led CV/profile review, authenticated LinkedIn editing and rendered-save validation.\n\n#### /Users/cooper/Projects/Mobius and /Users/cooper/Projects/ESGFrameworks\n\n- Mobius investigations and HERO controlled operations: Evidence Matrix, Langfuse, Axiom, WorkOS, Neon, `REPEATABLE READ READ ONLY`\n  - desc: Search project-specific blocks for production investigations, data exports, client correspondence and governed change boundaries.\n\n#### /Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees and local macOS storage\n\n- Disk cleanup and personal conservation: archived Codex JSONL,